WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.580 --> 00:00:10.019
Hello and welcome to this video on Navigation Invokers. So what is a Navigation Invoker?

2
00:00:10.019 --> 00:00:18.500
A Navigation Invoker generates a navigation mesh with the data on the fly around the actor

3
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:27.699
which the component is attached to. These are fantastic for dynamically generated worlds

4
00:00:27.859 --> 00:00:34.420
anywhere it's too large that you don't want to maintain a nav mesh for the entire world.

5
00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:41.780
It's great for open world games and it will just create a nav mesh around the specific

6
00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:52.579
actor which has this component on it. Usually a pawn. So let's go into the engine and have a

7
00:00:52.579 --> 00:00:59.619
look at how you would go about setting these up. All right, so here we are back in the engine.

8
00:00:59.619 --> 00:01:05.459
So if you want to set up Navigation Invokers, there's a couple of things that you need to do.

9
00:01:05.459 --> 00:01:12.180
So the first thing is I have an open world here and I have added a nav mesh balance volume.

10
00:01:12.799 --> 00:01:19.760
Okay, so that is going to still define where the nav mesh is created.

11
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:26.959
If you don't have this, there'll be no nav mesh created. So you can go away and then just

12
00:01:26.959 --> 00:01:31.519
add it to the entire world and it won't create a nav mesh for the entire world,

13
00:01:31.519 --> 00:01:38.959
it'll only create it at runtime dynamically around an Invoker. So feel free to make this

14
00:01:38.959 --> 00:01:47.680
big. Now what we need next is to change some of the project settings. So within navigation,

15
00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:51.840
in the project settings, the two things that you're looking for is, first of all,

16
00:01:51.840 --> 00:02:03.680
runtime generation. So under here we have nav mesh, runtime generation has to be dynamic.

17
00:02:05.069 --> 00:02:10.110
We're going to be building this at runtime quite a lot, so make sure that this is set

18
00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:28.110
to dynamic. Next up we have to set the generate navigation only around the Navigation Invokers.

19
00:02:28.669 --> 00:02:32.750
So those are the two settings that you have to change. You have to make sure the runtime

20
00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:41.150
generation is set to dynamic and that generating navigation only around the Invoker is checked.

21
00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:48.110
Okay, and once this is done, if you press the P key, you're not going to see anything any.

22
00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:54.430
So we're going to be able to unable to actually see when this is generated.

23
00:02:55.729 --> 00:03:01.090
Okay, now that means that we're going to have to jump into the engine to see this running.

24
00:03:02.129 --> 00:03:08.129
But we need to do one more thing, and that is any actor that you want navigation to be

25
00:03:08.129 --> 00:03:14.849
generated around, you have to add in a component. So here I have our BP top-down character,

26
00:03:15.409 --> 00:03:26.139
and I am going to add a nav navigation invoker. Here we see this, and that's going to add it

27
00:03:26.139 --> 00:03:33.259
to our character. And from this, I'm going to change some of the settings.

28
00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:40.060
So this navigation section here is important because it's going to do a couple of different

29
00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:47.740
things. The first thing is it's going to get us to define how far away we want to generate

30
00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:55.259
the tiles up to, and how far away a tile needs to be before it is removed from the generation.

31
00:03:55.900 --> 00:04:02.300
So you'll always want the removal radius to be bigger than the generation radius,

32
00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:10.060
and depending on your tile sizes, I'd make it relatively large. Okay, so by default it's set

33
00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:17.899
to 3000 and 5000. This is a bit big for our needs because we're not going to be able to see

34
00:04:17.899 --> 00:04:26.139
it relatively easily. So I'm going to cut this down to 500, and the removal at about 1000.

35
00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:37.410
Now under this we have supported agents, and this is going to reflect the agents which you've

36
00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:45.730
created within the project settings. But what is important is you're going to generate the nav

37
00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:53.250
mesh for any of these which are here. So standardly, you really want to

38
00:04:53.250 --> 00:05:00.540
untick any which are not relevant to the pawn that you're using. There are some other

39
00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:08.540
different cases. Sometimes you might want to use a navigation invoker on some other kind of actor,

40
00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:13.180
and you might want to have all of the supported agents. But because we're doing this

41
00:05:13.259 --> 00:05:18.379
around our character, and we know this character is going to fall into the default agent,

42
00:05:19.019 --> 00:05:27.019
I can untick the wide. I'll leave it ticked for the moment just so that we can see that it

43
00:05:27.019 --> 00:05:33.620
creates this. Okay, so that's up and running. Now I'm going to jump back into the engine,

44
00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:43.730
and I'm going to push play, and I'm going to push tilde, and then I'm going to type in

45
00:05:43.730 --> 00:05:53.490
show navigation, and that's going to bring up the navigation mesh. And you'll see here,

46
00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:59.810
if I click off this navigation mesh, the character cannot figure out the pathfinding.

47
00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:05.329
However, if I click on it, it can move, and the navigation mesh is going to update as I get

48
00:06:05.329 --> 00:06:12.290
closer. I've set this to quite a small number so that we can see it functioning. Now you'll

49
00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:16.129
see at the moment that I can't see the nav meshes which are coming up. So if I

50
00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:26.139
jump in and find our recast, select wide, and in the details panel, I'm going to tick so that it

51
00:06:26.139 --> 00:06:34.740
is drawn. Where is that? So enable drawing, and you can see that both of these nav meshes are

52
00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:43.459
getting drawn. So at any point where I'm running this, this is going to have to build two nav

53
00:06:43.459 --> 00:06:50.079
meshes, and one of them is never going to be used. So that is just wasted time. However,

54
00:06:50.079 --> 00:07:09.310
if I untick the wide, and run the game again, and again do show navigation, and

55
00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:16.660
enable drawing, you can see that it's now no longer created it. So it is important,

56
00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:23.620
especially if you're placing this on the Pawn, that you limit the navigation generation to just

57
00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:28.579
that Pawn. If you had a second creature which was large and fit in a different agent,

58
00:07:28.579 --> 00:07:35.060
you would set that nav mesh to generate as wider, and it would generate its own nav mesh,

59
00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:44.430
even if they overlap. So that is navigation in VOCUS. Usually you'd set them to a larger size,

60
00:07:45.149 --> 00:07:52.910
but very useful for larger worlds where you don't want to have to manage a very large nav

61
00:07:52.910 --> 00:08:02.670
mesh. Thank you very much.
